      <p>Orlando is a place where zoning is decisive. Florida licenses vacation rentals at the state level and requires tax registration, but local governments regulate where transient rentals are allowed. Within the City of Orlando and Orange County, and in nearby Osceola County, nightly rentals are permitted in some zoning districts and restricted or prohibited in others, and some ordinances date from before recent state legislation. Many homeowners associations forbid rentals shorter than a certain length. Purchasing a home in a neighborhood that looks like a vacation area is no guarantee of legal eligibility. Confirm the specific parcel's zoning status, any required local registration, and the association rules with the relevant city or county and the HOA, and revisit them before closing since rules can change.</p>

